\chapter{Library discovery and import state}
A runtime stores an ordered list of library roots. For a name such as
\api{printf}, discovery checks \api{root/printf/Package.mull} in order and
uses the first regular file. Names consist of ASCII letters, digits,
underscores, and hyphens. Path separators and traversal names are rejected.

\section{Configure the roots}
The default root is \api{mull/stdlib}, relative to the process working
directory. Supply a list to the constructor for explicit roots, or append
one with \api{addLibraryPath}. An empty list disables filesystem discovery
until a root is appended. Native registration and package discovery are
separate: registering a library does not create its package file.

\section{Caching and cycles}
A successful import must return a record. That record is cached by library
name for the lifetime of the runtime. Later imports return the cached value,
including stateful native functions such as the formatting sink. Changing
search paths does not reload an already cached package.

Imports in progress are tracked separately. Reentering an in-progress name
fails with a cyclic-import error. Failed imports are not cached, so a host can
repair a package or register its missing native implementation and retry.
If a discovered package is invalid, its error is reported; discovery does
not silently continue to a different package of the same name.

Package source obeys the same source-size, evaluation, call-depth, and frame
limits as other source. A nested import shares the caller's evaluation budget.
Successful imports clear \api{$E?}; failures replace it with the latest
diagnostic.
